<StoryLayout story={story}>
  <div class="container">
    <section class="section" id="the-remedy-they-chose">
      <h2><span class="num">01 — CONTEXT</span>The Remedy They Chose</h2>
      <p>On 18 September 2026, a class-action complaint filed in the Northern District of California quoted a sentence from a letter the frontier AI labs had published two months earlier: each company faces "intense competitive pressure not to unilaterally slow" development. The sentence was evidence of a cartel motive. Eight weeks later, a second complaint filed in San Francisco Superior Court quoted the same sentence from the same letter. This time, it was evidence that self-regulation fails.</p>
      <p>The letter was called "Pacing the Frontier." It appeared in July 2026, signed by senior executives of Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google — including Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei, OpenAI Chief Scientist Jakub Pachocki, OpenAI Chief Research Officer Mark Chen, and Google DeepMind co-founder Shane Legg. According to the Buist complaint, a working group of below-CEO representatives from the three companies had been meeting regularly since that month to create a standards body for the industry.</p>
      <p>On 12 September, Amodei published a personal essay titled "We Must Pace the Frontier." Its thesis was direct: "We must slow the pace at which we improve the capabilities of AI models." The essay proposed a three-step plan. The first step — embedding third-party evaluators inside each lab — Anthropic adopted unilaterally. The second step, which Amodei called "Democratic Coordination," required something different: "industry-wide coordination" to establish "common safety standards as well as limits on the rate of unchecked AI progress."</p>
      <p>Amodei flagged the legal obstacle himself. "For antitrust reasons, it's helpful for the US government to mediate or at least enable these discussions," he wrote. "They don't need to participate, but do need to issue a narrow waiver for certain kinds of safety conversations." His first footnote restated it: "With government mediation or waivers of antitrust restrictions."</p>
      <p>The convergence was immediate. According to the Buist complaint, Elon Musk quote-posted Amodei's announcement within approximately one hour and responded: "Dario is right." The complaint alleges Sam Altman wrote the same day: "I agree with Dario that we need to pace the frontier." Demis Hassabis, per the complaint, said the essay pointed toward "the right path forward." Two days later, the Buist complaint alleges, Altman stated that AI progress "should be slower than it otherwise could be" and that OpenAI would not wait for an antitrust exemption or legislation. The following day, according to the complaint, OpenAI Global Policy Chief Chris Lehane confirmed that OpenAI had been working with Anthropic and Google DeepMind on these issues for several weeks before Amodei's essay.</p>
      <p>No waiver existed. As the Buist complaint states: "No such waiver exists. Congress has enacted no exemption."</p>
