    </section>
    <section class="section" id="slowing-together">
      <h2><span class="num">02 — THE ANTITRUST SUIT</span>Slowing Together</h2>
      <p>Six days after Amodei's essay and the rapid-fire endorsements, the Buist plaintiffs — four consumers who, among them, pay for subscriptions to ChatGPT, Claude, Grok, and Gemini — filed a class-action complaint in the Northern District of California. <em>Buist v. Anthropic PBC</em>, No. 5:26-cv-10693-NC. The claim: one count under Section 1 of the Sherman Act, one count for injunctive relief under Section 16 of the Clayton Act. The defendants: Anthropic, OpenAI OpCo, SpaceXAI, and Google.</p>
      <p>The complaint frames the labs' coordinated pacing as a horizontal output-restricting cartel. Competitors agreed on how fast their products would improve — an agreement the complaint compares to any other arrangement among rivals to reduce product quality. The complaint alleges at paragraph 67 that coordinated pacing served the function of removing "the competitive penalty for restraint by ensuring that rivals restrain themselves too." The plaintiffs argue this agreement is unlawful per se, or alternatively under quick-look or rule-of-reason analysis. Their core contention: every safety objective the labs cite can be pursued unilaterally.</p>
      <p>The complaint alleges motive in the labs' own words. Paragraph 87(a): "slowing the pace of innovation was seen to be commercially untenable for any firm acting alone but advantageous if all slowed together." It alleges consciousness of antitrust risk: OpenAI asked members of Congress whether coordinating a slowdown could violate antitrust laws; Amodei wrote that the government would need to issue a waiver; Altman, per the complaint, said OpenAI would proceed without one.</p>
      <p>The requested injunction is specific. Paragraph 150 seeks to bar any agreement among horizontal competitors concerning the rate of development, limits on training compute, limits on AI-directed AI research imposed by agreement, coordinated delays, and capability checkpoints that function as horizontally agreed restraints — "as distinct from lawful governmental requirements or independent safety measures." Paragraph 151 states the carve-out plainly: the plaintiffs "do not seek to prohibit any Defendant from independently adopting safety measures or product guardrails, independently ... slowing or suspending its own development, ... complying with governmental requirements, [or] petitioning any governmental body."</p>
      <p>The line separating what the suit challenges from what it does not is the line between collective coordination and individual choice. As paragraph 12 puts it: "Whether frontier AI should develop more slowly is a question for each company acting alone, or for Congress and the agencies. It is not a question that four competitors may answer together."</p>
    </section>
<div class="phantom-wrap">
  <canvas class="frontier-freeze-suit-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
</div>
<p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Narrowing</strong> — Two walls advance from opposite sides of the canvas, slowly sealing a central passage; ambient particles compress and scatter at closure; two dim slits flicker at the far edges, barely passable, never enough.</p>
    <section class="section" id="slowing-themselves">
      <h2><span class="num">03 — THE FREEZE SUIT</span>Slowing Themselves</h2>
      <p>Eight weeks after the antitrust suit was filed, a different set of plaintiffs, in a different court, under a different legal theory, attacked the same industry from the opposite direction.</p>
      <p>On 12 November 2026, four nonprofit organizations — Irreplaceable Education Fund, Public Citizen, Heat Initiative, and Future Tech Insights (d/b/a The Alliance for Secure AI) — filed <em>Irreplaceable Education Fund et al. v. Anthropic PBC et al.</em> in San Francisco Superior Court. The single cause of action: a violation of California's Unfair Competition Law, Business and Professions Code section 17200, with a public-nuisance predicate. The defendant set is larger than in Buist and includes Meta, both OpenAI entities (OpenAI Group PBC and OpenAI Foundation), and both SpaceX entities (SpaceX Super Intelligence LLC and Corp). Meta was not named in the Buist suit.</p>
      <p>The Irreplaceable complaint does not mention antitrust law. It does not reference the Sherman Act, the Buist lawsuit, or any theory of coordinated pacing. Its argument runs in the opposite direction: the labs are developing systems they know are unsafe, and self-regulation has failed. "Nor is any industry granted the right to self-regulate the development of such dangerous products," paragraph 14 states. The complaint cites Amodei as estimating a roughly 25% chance that the future of AI goes "really, really badly." It cites Anthropic's amended S-1 registration statement as disclosing that "the scale of harm that could result from a catastrophic AI-related incident" may exceed what "the entire insurance industry combined" can underwrite. It cites David Robinson, who oversaw OpenAI's safety reports before resigning in October 2026, as stating: "I don't think that we or our peers — really, anyone in the industry — are being safe enough."</p>
      <p>And it quotes the same sentence from the same July letter. Paragraph 196 of the Irreplaceable complaint cites "Pacing the Frontier" for the admission that "each company — and country — is under intense competitive pressure not to unilaterally slow ... [AI] acceleration." In the Buist complaint, this language was evidence that competitors needed a cartel to escape the cost of restraint. In the Irreplaceable complaint, it is evidence that the labs will not restrain themselves.</p>
      <p>The requested relief is a freeze. Prayer A seeks "an immediate injunction forbidding Defendants from developing or deploying AI systems more capable than their most capable systems as of the date of this Complaint." Prayer B seeks "an immediate injunction forbidding Defendants from using AI systems to autonomously conduct or direct the research and development of more capable AI systems." The complaint was filed with a blank case number. No temporary restraining order was filed alongside it.</p>
      <p>Phil Aroneanu, founder of Irreplaceable Education Fund, said in a press release: "We must freeze frontier AI development until the technology is under control." In a separate interview with Al Jazeera, Aroneanu added: "A real freeze must specify what work has to halt, how compliance is verified, and the consequences of breaking the rules."</p>
      <p>The companies named in the Irreplaceable suit did not immediately respond to requests for comment, the AP reported.</p>
