    <section class="section" id="the-door-that-closed">
      <h2><span class="num">04 — THE MECHANISM</span>The Door That Closed</h2>
      <p>Both suits target the remedy the labs chose in September 2026: voluntary, coordinated self-pacing among competitors. Buist says the labs may not do it — antitrust law forbids horizontal agreements on the rate of product improvement. Irreplaceable says they cannot be trusted to do it — self-regulation is insufficient and the labs' own admissions prove they know the danger.</p>
      <p>The suits do not collide. They converge. Buist's requested injunction, at paragraphs 150(e) and 151, expressly exempts "lawful governmental requirements" and "independent safety measures" from its scope. The plaintiffs do not challenge a defendant for "complying with governmental requirements." A court-ordered freeze — which is what the Irreplaceable plaintiffs ask for — is a governmental requirement, not a horizontal agreement among competitors.</p>
      <p>What survives both legal theories? Two brakes. The first is unilateral restraint: a single lab choosing, on its own, to slow its development. The labs' own letter identified the problem with that path. Each company faces "intense competitive pressure not to unilaterally slow." The Buist complaint alleges that a firm which decelerates alone "loses customers, revenue, talent, and technological leadership." The second brake is outside compulsion: legislation, regulation, or a court order. Congress has not acted. No agency has mandated a slowdown. That absence is what led the Irreplaceable plaintiffs to a San Francisco courtroom, asking a state trial judge to supply the constraint that no other branch of government has imposed.</p>
      <p>Amodei anticipated this bind. His essay said the coordination step needed "government mediation or waivers of antitrust restrictions." According to the Buist complaint, Altman said OpenAI would not wait for one. No waiver materialized. The labs proceeded. The lawsuits followed.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="where-it-stands">
      <h2><span class="num">05 — STATUS</span>Where It Stands</h2>
