      <p>Neither case has a ruling on any substantive issue. No defendant has filed a public response to the merits of either complaint.</p>
      <p>In <em>Buist</em>, the docket through 13 November shows procedural activity but no answer or motion to dismiss on the merits from any defendant. OpenAI obtained a stipulation on 12 October extending its time to respond. SpaceXAI filed a motion on 13 October to sever the claims against it and transfer them to the Northern District of Texas, or alternatively to compel arbitration under its terms of service. The Buist plaintiffs opposed on 27 October, arguing that SpaceXAI had not established valid forum agreements with the named plaintiffs and that severance would fragment litigation of a single antitrust conspiracy. The motion was fully briefed by 3 November; a hearing is set for 18 November. On 13 November, a docket entry titled "Order on Motion to Transfer Case" appeared. Its text was not publicly available as of this writing. The case is assigned to Magistrate Judge Nathanael M. Cousins, with an initial case management conference set for 23 December.</p>
      <p>In <em>Irreplaceable</em>, the complaint was filed on 12 November with a blank case number. No temporary restraining order was filed concurrently. No defendant response has been located.</p>
      <p>The labs' chosen remedy — slowing together, voluntarily, without the antitrust waiver Amodei said was needed and Congress never provided — is now the subject of litigation from both directions. The brakes that survive both suits are the brakes the labs already told the public would not hold.</p>
    </section>
